window.docContents[255].resolve({"/Colorings/#section-memoization":{"contents":"To speed things up we build a list of colours [c 0, c 1, …, c (n - 1)] by ordinary\nstructural recursion on n, so that each colour is computed exactly once, and then\nlook up the entry we need. This is a simple form of memoization.\n\nTo read entries off a list we use List.getD: l.getD m d is the m-th entry of\nl (counting from 0) if m < l.length, and the default value d otherwise.\n\n#check List.getD\n\n\nTo compute the next colour we pass greedyChoice the partial colouring\nfun m => l.getD m 42, where l is the list built so far. As in greedy, the\ndefault value 42 is just filler: greedyChoice only looks at the colours of\nearlier vertices, and these are all entries of l.\n\nThe list of the first n greedy colours [c 0, c 1, …, c (n - 1)],\nbuilt by structural recursion./-- The list of the first n greedy colours [c 0, c 1, …, c (n - 1)],\nbuilt by structural recursion. -/\ndef greedyList : ℕ → List ℕ\n  | 0 => []\n  | n + 1 =>\n    let l := greedyList n\n    l ++ [G.greedyChoice (fun m => l.getD m 42) n]\n\n\nThe list greedyList n has length n.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma length_greedyList (n : ℕ) : (G.greedyList n).length = n := by\n  induction n <;> simp_all [greedyList]\n\n\nTo colour vertex n we build G.greedyList (n + 1) and use List.getD again to\nread off its last entry.\n\nThe memoized greedy colouring of G: build the list of the first n + 1\ncolours and look up the last entry./-- The memoized greedy colouring of G: build the list of the first n + 1\ncolours and look up the last entry. -/\ndef greedyMemo (n : ℕ) : ℕ :=\n  (G.greedyList (n + 1)).getD n 42\n\n\nWe now want to prove that this new version computes the same colours as greedy. The\nlist is correct: its m-th entry is greedy m. We use induction on the length of\nthe list: extending the list by one entry preserves the earlier entries\n(List.getD_append), and the new entry is the correct greedy choice by\ngreedyChoice_congr.\n\nlemma getD_greedyList {n m : ℕ} (hm : m < n) :\n    (G.greedyList n).getD m 42 = G.greedy m := by\n  induction n generalizing m with\n  | zero => grind\n  | succ n ih =>\n    rcases Nat.lt_succ_iff_lt_or_eq.mp hm with h | rfl\n    · rw [greedyList, List.getD_append _ _ _ _ (by simpa using h)]\n      exact ih h\n    · rw [greedyList, List.getD_append_right _ _ _ _ (by simp), length_greedyList,\n         Nat.sub_self, List.getD_cons_zero, greedy_eq_greedyChoice]\n      exact G.greedyChoice_congr fun k hk => ih hk\n\n\nlemma greedyMemo_eq_greedy (n : ℕ) : G.greedyMemo n = G.greedy n :=\n  G.getD_greedyList (Nat.lt_succ_self n)\n\n\nNow each colour is computed exactly once, so colouring vertex n makes only n + 1\ncalls to greedyChoice, whatever the graph. We can check this by tracing\ngreedyList as we traced greedy. Only the n + 1 branch calls greedyChoice, so\nthat is where we print the *.\n\nA copy of greedyList that prints one * per call to greedyChoice./-- A copy of greedyList that prints one * per call to greedyChoice. -/\ndef greedyListTraced : ℕ → List ℕ\n  | 0 => []\n  | n + 1 =>\n    dbg_trace \"*\";\n    let l := greedyListTraced n\n    l ++ [G.greedyChoice (fun m => l.getD m 42) n]\n\n\nTo colour vertex n we look up entry n of greedyListTraced (n + 1), just as\ngreedyMemo does. On the complete graph, colouring the vertices 0, 1, ..., 8 now\ntakes 1, 2, ..., 9 calls, rather than 1, 2, 4, ..., 256.\n\n#eval (List.range 9).mapM fun n =>\n  withTraceCount fun _ => ((⊤ : SimpleGraph ℕ).greedyListTraced (n + 1)).getD n 42\n\n\nThe number of calls is linear, although the running time is not, since appending to\na list and looking up its entries take time proportional to its length.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Colorings\u0009Greedy Colouring*\u0009Making  greedy  Efficient*","header":"7.8.6.2. Memoization","id":"/Colorings/#section-memoization"},"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-decidability":{"contents":"In classical logic the law of the excluded middle says that every proposition is\neither true or false. For proofs this is very useful: it allows us to reason by\ncases. In one branch of a proof we assume p holds and in the other we assume ¬p\nholds. But for computations this isn't very helpful: we want to know which branch\nto take.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Decidability, Finiteness and Degrees","header":"4.1. Decidability","id":"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-decidability"},"/Walks/#section-walks-in-the-mycielskian":{"contents":"Recall mμ(G), the mycielskian of a graph G. Given any non-nil walk in G,\nthere is a natural way to define a walk in mμ(G). We explore this construction in\ndetail below, using it to prove that every edge of G gives a 5-cycle in mμ(G),\nand that mμ(G) contains a Hamiltonian path or cycle whenever G does (provided\nG has at least two vertices).\n\nRather than defining the walks that give the Hamiltonian path and cycle directly, we\nfirst define a zig-zag walk, mycielskianZigZag, that has a simple recursive\ndefinition and from which it is easy to build paths and cycles in mμ(G) from paths\nand cycles in G.\n\nFirst, note that if u — w is a walk of length 1 in G (i.e. a single edge) then\nthere is a corresponding walk of length 2 in mμ(G), given by uˡ — wˡ — uʳ.\nFurther, if u — v — w is a walk of length 2 in G, then there is a corresponding\nwalk of length 4 in mμ(G): uˡ — vʳ — wˡ — vˡ — uʳ. Notice that this second\nexample can be obtained from the first by taking the walk corresponding to the single\nedge v — w, reversing it and adding the edges uˡ — vʳ and vˡ — uʳ to the\nstart and end respectively.\n\nThis generalizes, and allows us to define the walk mycielskianZigZag recursively.\nSuppose we have built the walk in mμ(G) from vˡ to vʳ corresponding to a\nnon-nil walk p : G.Walk v w, and let h : G.Adj u v. The walk for cons h p is\nthe edge uˡ — vʳ, then the reverse of the walk for p, then the edge vˡ — uʳ:\nmycielskianZigZag(cons h p) = uˡ — [mycielskianZigZag(p).reverse] — uʳ.\n\nGiven a non-nil walk from u to w in G, this is the zig-zag walk from uˡ\nto uʳ in mμ(G).section mycielskian\nvariable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V}\n\n/-- Given a non-nil walk from u to w in G, this is the zig-zag walk from uˡ\nto uʳ in mμ(G). -/\ndef Walk.mycielskianZigZag {u w : V} {p : G.Walk u w} (hn : ¬p.Nil) :\n    mμ(G).Walk uˡ uʳ :=\n  match p with\n  | nil => (hn .nil).elim  -- This case is ruled out by the ¬p.Nil hypothesis\n  | cons' u v w h p =>\n    -- the last edge of the walk vˡ — uʳ\n    have hl : mμ(G).Adj vˡ uʳ := h.symm\n    match p with\n    | nil => cons' _ vˡ _ h <| cons hl nil -- uˡ — vˡ — uʳ\n    | cons h' p' =>\n      have hn' : ¬(p'.cons h').Nil := nofun\n      cons' _ vʳ _ h ((mycielskianZigZag hn').reverse.concat hl)\n\n\nThe two cases of the walk mycielskianZigZag: cons h nil (left) and cons h (cons h' p') (right)\n\nIf we had marked the definition of mycielskianZigZag with the @[simp] attribute\nthen its automatically generated equational lemmas would be available for simp to\nuse with names like mycielskianZigZag.eq_1. We instead state these as simp lemmas\nthat have more meaningful names.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma Walk.mycielskianZigZag_cons_nil {u v} (h : G.Adj u v)\n    (hn : ¬(cons h nil).Nil) : mycielskianZigZag hn = cons (by simpa using h)\n      (cons (by simpa using h.symm : mμ(G).Adj vˡ uʳ) nil) := rfl\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma Walk.mycielskianZigZag_cons_cons {u v x w} (h : G.Adj u v) (h' : G.Adj v x)\n    (p' : G.Walk x w) (hn : ¬(cons h (cons h' p')).Nil) :\n    mycielskianZigZag hn = cons (by simpa using h)\n      ((mycielskianZigZag (nofun : ¬(cons h' p').Nil)).reverse.concat\n        (by simpa using h.symm)) := rfl\n\n\nFrom the definition of mycielskianZigZag, Lean automatically generates a\nfunctional induction principle mycielskianZigZag.induct. It has one case for each\nmatch case of the definition, named case1 and case2, whose variables are the\npattern variables of that case, together with an induction hypothesis wherever the\ndefinition calls itself. We use it with\ninduction hn using mycielskianZigZag.induct: its cases follow the definition\nexactly, which plain induction on the walk does not.\n\n#check mycielskianZigZag.induct -- slightly scary looking! (but useful)\n\n\nThe zig-zag walk is twice as long as the original walk.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma Walk.length_mycielskianZigZag {u w} {p : G.Walk u w} (hn : ¬p.Nil) :\n    (mycielskianZigZag hn).length = 2 * p.length := by\n  induction hn using mycielskianZigZag.induct with\n  | case1 => rfl\n  | case2 =>\n    grind [length_cons, mycielskianZigZag_cons_cons, length_concat, length_reverse]\n\n\nWe can use the zig-zag walk to map any non-nil walk of length k in G to a\nwalk of length 2k + 2 in the Mycielskian. This preserves the property of being a\npath and in particular maps Hamiltonian paths to Hamiltonian paths. We simply add the\ntwo edges uʳ — none — wʳ at the end of the zig-zag walk.\n\nGiven a non-nil walk from u to w in G, this is a walk from uˡ to\nwʳ in mμ(G). If the original walk was a (Hamiltonian) path then so is the new\nwalk./-- Given a non-nil walk from u to w in G, this is a walk from uˡ to\nwʳ in mμ(G). If the original walk was a (Hamiltonian) path then so is the new\nwalk. -/\nprotected def Walk.mycielskian {u w} {p : Walk G u w} (hn : ¬p.Nil) :\n    mμ(G).Walk uˡ wʳ :=\n  mycielskianZigZag hn |>.concat (by simp) |>.concat (by simp : mμ(G).Adj none _)\n\n\nConverting a path in G to one in its Mycielskian mμ(G)\n\nUsing this walk we can also map closed walks in G to closed walks in the\nMycielskian. This preserves the property of being a cycle and in particular maps\nHamiltonian cycles to Hamiltonian cycles in the Mycielskian.\n\nGiven a closed walk from u to u in G, there is a walk from uʳ to uʳ\nin mμ(G). If the original walk was a (Hamiltonian) cycle then so is this new walk./-- Given a closed walk from u to u in G, there is a walk from uʳ to uʳ\nin mμ(G). If the original walk was a (Hamiltonian) cycle then so is this new walk.\n-/\ndef Walk.mycielskianClosed {u} (c : Walk G u u) : mμ(G).Walk uʳ uʳ :=\n  match c with\n  | nil => nil\n  | cons h' nil => (h'.ne rfl).elim\n  | cons' _ v _ h <| cons' _ w _ h' p =>\n    Walk.mycielskian (not_nil_cons (h := h') (p := p)) |>.cons (by simpa using h)\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma Walk.mycielskianClosed_nil {u} :\n    mycielskianClosed (nil : Walk G u u) = nil := rfl\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma Walk.mycielskianClosed_cons_cons {u v w} (h : G.Adj u v) (h' : G.Adj v w)\n    (p : Walk G w u) : mycielskianClosed (cons h (cons h' p)) =\n      (Walk.mycielskian (not_nil_cons (h := h') (p := p))).cons\n        (by simpa using h) := rfl\n\n\nConverting a cycle in G to one in its Mycielskian mμ(G)\n\nThe Walk.mycielskian of a non-nil walk p has length 2 * p.length + 2.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma Walk.length_mycielskian {u w} (p : Walk G u w) (hn : ¬p.Nil) :\n    (p.mycielskian hn).length = 2 * p.length + 2 := by\n  simp [Walk.mycielskian]\n\n\nThe Walk.mycielskianClosed of a non-nil closed walk c has length\n2 * c.length + 1.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma Walk.length_mycielskianClosed {u} {c : Walk G u u} (hn : ¬c.Nil) :\n    c.mycielskianClosed.length = 2 * c.length + 1 :=\n  match c with\n  | nil => (hn .nil).elim\n  | cons h' nil => (h'.ne rfl).elim\n  | cons h (cons h' p) => by\n    grind [mycielskianClosed, length_cons, length_mycielskian]\n\n\nOur next goal is to show that the Walk.mycielskian preserves the property of being\na path. It will be useful to first establish some simple properties of the zig-zag\nwalk mycielskianZigZag.\n\nThe zig-zag walk only visits left or right copies of vertices from the original\nwalk.\n\nlemma Walk.exists_of_mem_support_mycielskianZigZag {u w z} {p : G.Walk u w}\n    (hn : ¬p.Nil) (hz : z ∈ (mycielskianZigZag hn).support) :\n    ∃ y ∈ p.support, z = yˡ ∨ z = yʳ := by\n  induction hn using mycielskianZigZag.induct <;> aesop\n\n\nIn particular, the zig-zag walk does not visit none.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma Walk.none_notMem_support_mycielskianZigZag {u w} {p : G.Walk u w}\n    (hn : ¬p.Nil) :\n    none ∉ (mycielskianZigZag hn).support := by\n  intro h_in\n  obtain ⟨y, _, hy⟩ := exists_of_mem_support_mycielskianZigZag hn h_in\n  cases hy <;> contradiction\n\n\nIf the original walk p : G.Walk u w is a path, then the zig-zag walk does not\nvisit wʳ.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma Walk.end_right_notMem_support_mycielskianZigZag {u w} {p : G.Walk u w}\n    (hn : ¬p.Nil) (hp : p.IsPath) : wʳ ∉ (mycielskianZigZag hn).support := by\n  induction hn using mycielskianZigZag.induct <;> aesop\n\n\nThe second vertex of the Walk.mycielskian is in the zig-zag walk.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma Walk.snd_mem_support_mycielskianZigZag {u w} {p : G.Walk u w} (hn : ¬p.Nil) :\n  (p.mycielskian hn).snd ∈ (mycielskianZigZag hn).support := by\n  induction hn using mycielskianZigZag.induct\n    <;> simp_all [Walk.mycielskian]\n\n\nIf the original walk p : G.Walk u w is a path, then the zig-zag walk is also a\npath.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma Walk.IsPath.mycielskianZigZag {u w} {p : G.Walk u w} (hp : p.IsPath)\n    (hn : ¬p.Nil) : (mycielskianZigZag hn).IsPath := by\n  apply IsPath.mk'\n  induction hn using mycielskianZigZag.induct with\n  | case1 u v h hl hn => simpa using h.ne\n  | case2 u v h hl w x h' p' hn hnp ih =>\n    -- grind can solve this with a little help\n    grind [mycielskianZigZag_cons_cons, support_concat, support_reverse, isPath_def,\n      exists_of_mem_support_mycielskianZigZag hnp]\n\n\nIf the original walk p : G.Walk u w is a path, then the Walk.mycielskian is a\npath.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma Walk.IsPath.mycielskian {u w} {p : G.Walk u w} (hp : p.IsPath) (hn : ¬p.Nil) :\n    (p.mycielskian hn).IsPath := by\n  apply IsPath.mk'\n  have := (hp.mycielskianZigZag hn).support_nodup\n  grind [Walk.mycielskian, Walk.concat, end_right_notMem_support_mycielskianZigZag,\n    support_append, support_nil, none_notMem_support_mycielskianZigZag]\n\n\nIf the original walk p : G.Walk u u is a cycle then the Walk.mycielskianClosed is\na cycle. (In fact this holds under the weaker assumption that p is not nil and\np.support.tail contains no repeated vertices.)\n\nlemma Walk.isCycle_mycielskianClosed {u} {p : Walk G u u} (hn : ¬p.Nil)\n    (hc : p.support.tail.Nodup) : (mycielskianClosed p).IsCycle :=\n  match p with\n  | nil => (hn .nil).elim\n  | cons h' nil => (h'.ne rfl).elim\n  -- we use the cons' pattern here to make the InfoView more readable.\n  | cons' _ v _ h (cons' _ w _ h' p) => by\n    -- we now use cons_isCycle_iff_length_ne_one that we proved earlier to reduce\n    -- this to showing that the Walk.mycielskian is a path of length not equal to 1\n    rw [mycielskianClosed, cons_isCycle_iff_length_ne_one]\n    -- We know that the Walk.mycielskian of a path is a path and simp\n    -- can take care of the length.\n    exact ⟨(IsPath.mk' hc).mycielskian (by simp), by simp⟩\n\n\nThe weaker assumption is exactly what we need for one application of the result. If\nh : G.Adj u v then the closed walk u — v — u goes along an edge and straight\nback, so it is not a cycle. But it is not nil, and the tail [v, u] of its support\nhas no repeated vertices, so its image uʳ — vˡ — uˡ — vʳ — none — uʳ is a\n5-cycle. Hence every edge of G gives a 5-cycle in mμ(G). We will use this to\nfind the bridges of mμ(G) in Bridges and to compute its girth in\nGirth of the Mycielskian.\n\nlemma isCycle_mycielskianClosed_of_adj {u v : V} (h : G.Adj u v) :\n    (mycielskianClosed (cons h (cons h.symm nil))).IsCycle :=\n  isCycle_mycielskianClosed (by simp) (by simp [h.ne.symm])\n\n\nIf the original walk p : G.Walk u w is a Hamiltonian path, then the\nWalk.mycielskian is a Hamiltonian path.\n\nprotected lemma Walk.IsHamiltonian.mycielskian [DecidableEq V] {u w} {p : G.Walk u w}\n    (hp : p.IsHamiltonian) (hn : ¬p.Nil) : (p.mycielskian hn).IsHamiltonian := by\n  have : Fintype V := hp.fintype\n  refine isHamiltonian_iff_isPath_and_length_eq.2 ⟨hp.isPath.mycielskian hn, ?_⟩\n  grind [length_mycielskian, Fintype.card_option, Fintype.card_sum, hp.length_eq,\n    not_nil_iff_lt_length]\n\n\nIf the original walk p : G.Walk u u is a Hamiltonian cycle, then the\nWalk.mycielskianClosed is a Hamiltonian cycle.\n\nlemma Walk.IsHamiltonianCycle.mycielskianClosed [DecidableEq V] {u} {p : G.Walk u u}\n    (hp : p.IsHamiltonianCycle) : p.mycielskianClosed.IsHamiltonianCycle := by\n  have : Fintype V := hp.isHamiltonian_tail.fintype\n  refine isHamiltonianCycle_iff_isCycle_and_length_eq.2\n    ⟨isCycle_mycielskianClosed hp.isCycle.not_nil hp.isCycle.support_nodup, ?_⟩\n  grind [length_mycielskianClosed hp.isCycle.not_nil, hp.length_eq,\n        Fintype.card_option, Fintype.card_sum]\n\n\nFinally we can show that if G is Hamiltonian then mμ(G) is also Hamiltonian\n(unless G has only one vertex, in which case mμ(G) is an edge plus an isolated\nvertex and so is clearly not Hamiltonian).\n\ntheorem IsHamiltonian.mycielskian [DecidableEq V] [Fintype V]\n    (hH : G.IsHamiltonian) (hf : Fintype.card V ≠ 1) : mμ(G).IsHamiltonian := by\n  intro\n  have ⟨_, _, hc⟩ := hH hf\n  exact ⟨_, _, hc.mycielskianClosed⟩\n\nend mycielskian\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Walks","header":"8.8. Walks in the Mycielskian","id":"/Walks/#section-walks-in-the-mycielskian"}});